From: the SK-D840N 7.3 line
Subject: [PATCH] net: zx279133: the PPU has to run this board's own microcode

The 7.3 tree's driver compares the microcode blob's CRC32 against one
value, 0xef1d7647, and that value belongs to the SR1010: the same SoC on
a different board.  This board's own blob is 0xc839b946 - the vendor's
/etc/mcode/mcode_133.bin, read out of this board's own flash.  The two
carry the same name and the same size (103,456 B) and differ in bytes.

A PPU running the SR1010's blob does not forward this board's frames.
They stall inside the PPU: the driver loads the blob, the PPU reports a
version, every netdev's counters stay at zero and the board's ARP table
stays empty.  The measurement is in docs/75: a 7500-frame flood on this
board entered the PPU 427 times with the SR1010 blob and 7625 times with
this board's, and was forwarded 0 times in both cases without this
board's blob.

The port-flow table is in the blob as well, and that one was the SR1010's
too: the 48 ports its PORT_FLOW tags name differ from the ones the
driver's own table has (ports 44 to 54 and 63).  This board's blob's
table and the driver's table agree on all 48, so with this board's blob
the hardware gets this board's topology.

So the 7.3 line has been shipping another board's firmware and calling it
calibrated.  This patch gives the driver the two constants the 6.18
generation already has - the board's own CRC, and the CRC of the one blob
the RX-hash hook may be installed on - and makes the driver say which one
it got:

  * The RX-hash hook rewrites four instructions inside a blob's
    instruction stream, so it is only installed when the blob is the one
    it was written against.  With this board's blob the hook stays off and
    the native RX queues are used, which is the 6.18 generation's
    behaviour, and the datapath has a path for it: the RSS setup reports
    -EOPNOTSUPP and restores native RX.
  * A blob that is not this board's own is now refused in words rather
    than in counters.  The driver says the PPU will not forward frames,
    which is what actually happens.

The VID-key calibration belongs to the same blob as the RX-hash hook: it
rewrites two instructions and expects three more to be exactly what that
blob carries, so it refuses to load any other firmware.  That refusal
stays, and it now says why.  On this board the parameter is off - the
image has no /etc/modules.d, the driver is built in, and the parameter
takes its default of false - so the blob this board needs is accepted.

The single-step capture reads and writes the PPU's instruction stream at
offsets that belong to one blob, so it is refused for any other firmware,
as before, with the reason written down.

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-np.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-np.c
index 95585c2..733a27f 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-np.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-np.c
@@ -2421,7 +2421,13 @@ static int zx279133_ppu_capture_mode(struct zx279133_eth *eth, u16 pc,
 	default:
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 	}
-	if (READ_ONCE(eth->ppu_firmware_crc32) != 0xef1d7647)
+	/*
+	 * The single-step capture reads and writes the PPU's instruction
+	 * stream, and the offsets it knows are the ones of one blob.  With
+	 * any other firmware they mean nothing, so the capture is refused
+	 * rather than pointed at the wrong instruction.
+	 */
+	if (READ_ONCE(eth->ppu_firmware_crc32) != ZX279133_MCODE_RX_HASH_CRC)
 		return -ESTALE;
 	*selected = 0xff;
 	context->key_rc = -ENODATA;
@@ -2678,12 +2684,24 @@ static int zx279133_ppu_mcode_prepare(struct zx279133_eth *eth)
 		goto err_fw;
 	first = ALIGN(first, 4);
 	fw_crc = crc32_le(~0U, fw->data, fw->size) ^ ~0U;
-	hash = rx_hash && fw->size == 103456 && fw_crc == 0xef1d7647;
+	hash = rx_hash && fw->size == 103456 && fw_crc == ZX279133_MCODE_RX_HASH_CRC;
 	if (rx_hash && !hash)
-		dev_warn(eth->dev, "uncalibrated PPU firmware; retaining native RX queues\n");
+		dev_info(eth->dev,
+			 "the RX-hash hook belongs to another PPU firmware; the native RX queues stay\n");
+	if (fw->size == 103456 && fw_crc != ZX279133_MCODE_BOARD_CRC)
+		dev_warn(eth->dev,
+			 "the PPU firmware is not this board's own (crc32 0x%08x, expected 0x%08x): the PPU will not forward frames\n",
+			 fw_crc, ZX279133_MCODE_BOARD_CRC);
 
+	/*
+	 * The VID-key calibration belongs to the blob the RX-hash hook was
+	 * calibrated against: it rewrites two instructions and expects three
+	 * more to be exactly what that blob carries.  Any other firmware has
+	 * nothing to calibrate against, so the load is refused rather than
+	 * write a VID-key layout into a firmware that does not implement it.
+	 */
 	if (vlan_offload &&
-	    (fw_crc != 0xef1d7647 || fw->size != 103456 ||
+	    (fw_crc != ZX279133_MCODE_RX_HASH_CRC || fw->size != 103456 ||
 	     get_unaligned_be64(fw->data + 16 + 139 * 8) !=
 					0x0010c0000000001fULL ||
 	     get_unaligned_be64(fw->data + 16 + 650 * 8) !=
@@ -2691,7 +2709,8 @@ static int zx279133_ppu_mcode_prepare(struct zx279133_eth *eth)
 	     get_unaligned_be64(fw->data + 16 + 582 * 8) !=
 					0x0704c00018680523ULL)) {
 		release_firmware(fw);
-		return -ESTALE;
+		return dev_err_probe(eth->dev, -ESTALE,
+				     "the VID-key calibration belongs to another PPU firmware\n");
 	}
 	agclk = readl(eth->pps_base + ZX279133_PPU_AGCLK_CFG);
 	if (agclk & ZX279133_PPU_CORE_AGCLK)
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
index bea2b64..9ba35bc 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
@@ -370,6 +370,31 @@ struct led_trigger;
 #define ZX279133_MCODE_TAG_VERSION	0xaaaaaaaa
 #define ZX279133_MCODE_TAG_PORT_FLOW	0xbbbbbbbb
 
+/*
+ * The CRC32 of this board's own PPU microcode.
+ * 
+ * Two blobs carry the name zte/zx279133/mcode_intel.bin and the size
+ * 103,456 B, and they differ in bytes.  0xc839b946 is this board's own:
+ * the vendor's /etc/mcode/mcode_133.bin, read out of this board's flash.
+ * 0xef1d7647 is the SR1010's - the same SoC on a different board.  A PPU
+ * running the SR1010's blob does not forward this board's frames: they
+ * stall inside the PPU and no netdev ever counts a byte (docs/75, measured
+ * with a 7500-frame flood on this board).  Nothing about that failure looks
+ * like a firmware problem from the outside - the driver loads the blob, the
+ * PPU reports a version, and the counters simply stop - so the driver
+ * checks the CRC and says so.
+ */
+#define ZX279133_MCODE_BOARD_CRC	0xc839b946
+/*
+ * The CRC32 of the one blob the RX-hash hook may be installed on.
+ * 
+ * The hook rewrites four instructions at an offset inside the blob's
+ * instruction stream, so it only means anything in the blob it was
+ * written against.  The vendor does not patch this board's blob either:
+ * the vendor's PPU takes its hash from the SE hardware hash table.
+ */
+#define ZX279133_MCODE_RX_HASH_CRC	0xef1d7647
+
 #define ZX279133_IDM_BASE		0x280000
 #define ZX279133_IDM_TX_BASE		0x0004
 #define ZX279133_IDM_TX_DOORBELL_COUNT_SHIFT	17
